# Pick the theme from the terminal background. Plain `auto`, never
# `auto:always`: the detection is an OSC 11 query written straight to the
# terminal, so it only works when bat is the program holding it. Asking anyway
# while the output is piped (fzf previews, lazygit, MANPAGER) costs a one
# second timeout, paints the query over whatever is on screen, and still ends
# up on Monokai, because the reply goes to the program reading the terminal.
# Those callers get the theme from BAT_THEME instead, which wins over this and
# which bash sets once at startup (bash/bashrc.d/colors.sh). PowerShell passes
# --theme on the command line for the same reason (powershell/CliTools.ps1).
--theme=auto
--theme-dark="everforest-dark-medium"
--theme-light="everforest-light-medium"

# Everforest marks comments and keywords in italics; the JetBrains Mono Nerd
# Font has them.
--italic-text=always

# Line numbers, git change markers and a file header; no grid. The `cat`
# replacement in the shells and the fzf previews choose their own --style.
--style=numbers,changes,header-filename,snip

# Files bat would otherwise show as plain text.
--map-syntax=*.kdl:Rust
--map-syntax=.ideavimrc:VimL
--map-syntax=*vsvimrc:VimL
--map-syntax=*.ps1xml:XML
--map-syntax="fzfrc:Bourne Again Shell (bash)"
